## 2. `collectSnippets` as a pure, testable function

Move the branch-walking + extraction out of `index.ts` into `snippets.ts` (or a new `collect.ts`) as a pure function that takes the entries and returns `Snippet[]`. Per your note, **walk the branch in order and extract — no timestamp sorting**.

```ts
// snippets.ts
import type { SessionEntry } from "@mariozechner/pi-coding-agent";

export function collectSnippets(
  entries: SessionEntry[],
  opts: { includeInline: boolean; limit: number },
): Snippet[] {
  const snippets: Snippet[] = [];
  let startId = 0;

  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (snippets.length >= opts.limit) break;
    if (entry.type !== "message") continue;
    const msg = entry.message;
    if (msg.role !== "assistant") continue;

    const text = extractText(msg.content);
    if (!text) continue;

    const sourceLabel = formatTimestamp(entry.timestamp); // e.g. HH:MM:SS
    const found = extractSnippets(text, entry.id, sourceLabel, startId, opts.includeInline, opts.limit);
    snippets.push(...found);
    startId += found.length;
  }

  return snippets;
}
```

Key points:
- `extractText` and `extractSnippets` already live in `snippets.ts`, so this composes cleanly.
- `sourceLabel` is derived from `entry.timestamp` (the `HH:MM:SS` you see in the picker), not used for ordering.
- `index.ts` then just does: `const snippets = collectSnippets(ctx.sessionManager.getBranch(), { includeInline, limit });`

This makes the extraction logic unit-testable without a live `ExtensionCommandContext`.

